Source: mptcp-bpf-dscp
Section: net
Priority: optional
Maintainer: Yannick Chabanois <contact@openmptcprouter.com>
Build-Depends: debhelper-compat (= 13), clang, llvm, libbpf-dev, linux-libc-dev
Standards-Version: 4.6.2
Homepage: https://www.openmptcprouter.com
Rules-Requires-Root: no

Package: mptcp-bpf-dscp
Architecture: any
# No ${shlibs:Depends}: the installed artifact is a BPF-target ELF object
# loaded by bpftool/the kernel, not a dynamically-linked host executable -
# dh_shlibdeps has nothing to compute for it (verified: dpkg-gencontrol
# warns "used but not defined" if it's left in).
Depends: ${misc:Depends}, bpftool, procps
Description: MPTCP BPF DSCP-aware scheduler (server/VPS side)
 BPF struct_ops MPTCP scheduler that pins a DSCP traffic class to a
 chosen WAN subflow, either by local endpoint IP (router-side, see the
 OpenWrt mptcp-bpf-dscp package in openmptcprouter-feeds) or by MPTCP
 remote endpoint id (server/VPS-side: every subflow here shares the
 same local IP, so pins key on the address id the router assigned to
 the originating WAN instead).
 .
 This is the Debian/server counterpart of that OpenWrt package: same
 BPF program, packaged to run on the VPS half of an OpenMPTCProuter
 multipath link. Ships a systemd unit that loads and pins the
 scheduler at boot and sets it as net.mptcp.scheduler. Install
 mptcp-dscp-manager to actually configure DSCP pins.

Package: mptcp-dscp-manager
Architecture: all
Depends: ${misc:Depends}, bpftool, mptcp-bpf-dscp (= ${source:Version})
Description: CLI to manage the mptcp-bpf-dscp scheduler's DSCP pins
 mptcp-scheduler-dscp.sh: show/set/del DSCP class pins in the
 mptcp-bpf-dscp scheduler's BPF maps.
 .
 On this (server/VPS) package, use 'mptcp-scheduler-dscp.sh set <dscp>
 id <N>' where <N> is the MPTCP endpoint id the router assigned to the
 WAN that should carry <dscp> (network.<iface>.ip4table on the router,
 kept stable across reconnects). The router-side 'set <dscp>
 <interface>' form also works here but is normally useless on a VPS
 with a single local IP.
